[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Memory Management and Leak Detection in NativeScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"NativeScript bridges JavaScript and the native mobile runtimes (iOS/Android). While this allows you to write cross-platform apps using JavaScript or TypeScript, it also means you need to be mindful of how memory is allocated and released across the bridge. A memory leak occurs when objects that are no longer needed by the application are still referenced somewhere, preventing the Garbage Collector (GC) from freeing up their memory."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the NativeScript Bridge"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Every time you create a UI component in NativeScript (like a Button or a Label), a corresponding native object is created on the native platform (e.g., android.widget.Button on Android or UIButton on iOS). The JavaScript object holds a reference to the native object, and vice versa. If either side retains a reference improperly, a memory leak happens, eventually causing sluggish performance or out-of-memory crashes."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Common Causes of Memory Leaks"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Global event listeners or subscriptions that are never unsubscribed when a page is unloaded."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Retaining strong references to Page or View instances inside closures or singleton services."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Circular references between JavaScript modules and native view controllers."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Prevention"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Always clean up event listeners and timers inside the unloaded lifecycle event of your NativeScript pages or components. Here is an example of proper cleanup in TypeScript:"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"import { Page, NavigatedData } from '@nativescript/core';

function onNavigatedTo(args: NavigatedData) {
    const page = <Page>args.object;
    // Add event listener
    page.on('loaded', myCustomHandler);
}

function onUnloaded(args: NavigatedData) {
    const page = <Page>args.object;
    // Clean up to prevent memory leak
    page.off('loaded', myCustomHandler);
}

function myCustomHandler() {
    console.log('Page loaded');
}

exports.onNavigatedTo = onNavigatedTo;
exports.onUnloaded = onUnloaded;"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Detecting Memory Leaks"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"NativeScript provides built-in instrumentation to track leaked pages and views. You can enable heap tracing and check for detached UI elements using the core profiling tools or by monitoring the application output logs when navigating back and forth between pages."},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"To detect leaks programmatically during development, you can listen to garbage collection events or use platform-specific profilers like Android Studio Profiler or Xcode Instruments to capture heap snapshots and inspect retained object graphs."}]
